window.LESSON = {"phases": ["Understand people", "Guide the work", "Judge fairly", "Communicate", "Empower & review"], "segments": [{"id": "welcome", "title": "Help people contribute with purpose.", "short": "Leading in everyday work", "chapter": "SECTION 5 / LEADING", "phase": 0, "takeaway": "Leading connects people’s motivation, judgment, communication, and collective work to a shared purpose.", "narration": "Welcome to Section Five: Leading. Human resources systems help people enter and develop in their roles. Leading concerns how people are guided, motivated, and supported in everyday work. At our fictional Cedar Crossing Library, a small project group is preparing a community history display with photographs, audio clips, and written captions. The staff bring different experience and different concerns. Producing a useful display will require more than assigning tasks. We will examine motivation, leadership, ethics, communication, and teamwork through this invented project. The recurring diagram asks us to understand people, guide the work, judge fairly, communicate, and empower with review. These are connected questions, not a fixed textbook formula. The assessed Self-Managed Team Experiment is a separate case. This optional lesson supports Chapters Thirteen through Seventeen. You can listen, read, revisit, or skip practice. Your last chapter is remembered on this browser; nothing is submitted to Canvas.", "description": "An original illustration shows a small group preparing a fictional community history display with photographs, an audio station, and captions. A five-part teaching diagram connects understanding people, guiding work, ethical judgment, communication, and empowerment with review.", "explore": [["Read alongside your textbook", "Use your own print textbook, digital textbook, or PDF for Chapters 13–17. The supplied course instructions say the assigned textbook readings are not provided in Canvas. Follow exact chapter titles and section headings because page numbering differs across formats."], ["A fictional learning setting", "Cedar Crossing, its display, people, and proposed actions are invented. The project is a temporary collaboration, not a pilot to replace a reference department’s management. It does not represent UNT or Riverside Public Library. No fictional result is presented as measured evidence."], ["Your reading route", "Chapter 13 is Motivation in the Workplace; Chapter 14, Organizational Leadership; Chapter 15, Professional Ethics; Chapter 16, Organizational Communication; Chapter 17, Empowering Employees through the Use of Teams."]]}, {"id": "motivation", "title": "Ask what makes the work meaningful and workable.", "short": "Needs and motivation", "chapter": "CHAPTER 13 / MOTIVATION IN THE WORKPLACE", "phase": 0, "takeaway": "Motivation theories offer lenses for inquiry. They do not reveal a person’s needs without evidence.", "narration": "Motivation concerns what energizes, directs, and sustains effort. Content theories ask which needs or features of work matter. Maslow's hierarchy draws attention to needs from basic security through belonging, esteem, and self-fulfillment. It is a model, not a reliable ladder for diagnosing each colleague. Herzberg distinguishes hygiene factors, such as working conditions and supervision, from motivators connected to achievement, recognition, responsibility, and the work itself. In that model, removing dissatisfaction does not automatically create satisfaction. That is not a reason to neglect pay or conditions. At Cedar Crossing, repairing an unreliable audio workstation removes a barrier. Giving an interested employee meaningful responsibility for shaping the listening experience offers a different kind of opportunity. Job enrichment adds meaningful scope and discretion, rather than simply more tasks. Ask what the person values and what resources the task requires. People can value stability, learning, relationships, or accomplishment differently, and their priorities can change. A theory helps frame questions; conversation and evidence help answer them.", "description": "Two distinct panels show workable conditions and meaningful work. Repairing an audio station illustrates removal of a barrier; responsibility for shaping the listening experience illustrates enrichment. Neither is a guaranteed prediction about a particular worker.", "explore": [["Content theories in Chapter 13", "Maslow distinguishes physiological, safety, social, esteem, and self-actualization needs. McClelland examines learned needs for achievement, affiliation, and power. These frameworks help organize questions, not label a person permanently."], ["Herzberg’s distinction", "Hygiene or maintenance factors concern the work context: pay, security, working conditions, supervision, policy, and relationships. Motivators concern job content: achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, and advancement. The textbook notes inconclusive research. Treat the distinction as a lens, not proof that money never matters or that all employees respond identically."], ["Job enrichment", "Job enrichment increases meaningful responsibility and opportunities to use capabilities. More repetitive tasks alone are job enlargement, not necessarily enrichment."]]}, {"id": "expectancy", "title": "Check every link between effort and a valued outcome.", "short": "Expectancy and fairness", "chapter": "CHAPTER 13 / MOTIVATION IN THE WORKPLACE", "phase": 0, "takeaway": "Ask whether effort can produce performance, performance can produce an outcome, and that outcome matters.", "narration": "Process theories ask how people decide where to put effort. Vroom's expectancy theory connects three perceptions. Expectancy asks whether effort can produce the needed performance. Instrumentality asks whether that performance will lead to an outcome. Valence asks how much the person values that outcome. The model multiplies these factors, so one missing link can undermine the whole connection. Imagine an employee who wants to learn audio editing but has neither working software nor practice time. Promising more recognition does not repair the effort-to-performance link. Clear instructions, equipment, and supported practice might help. Even with those in place, a promised opportunity must be credible and valued. Equity theory adds a different question: how does someone judge their contributions and outcomes compared with a relevant other? Perceived unfairness can matter even when a manager believes the arrangement is reasonable. Investigate the comparison and the circumstances. Neither formula nor comparison lets us calculate a colleague's actual motivation from a distance.", "description": "A labeled chain links effort to performance through expectancy, then performance to outcome through instrumentality; valence labels the value of the outcome. A missing-software example highlights the first link. Equity is shown separately as a perceived comparison of contributions and outcomes.", "explore": [["Vroom’s model", "Motivational force = expectancy × instrumentality × valence in the textbook model. Figure 13.6 clarifies expectancy as effort leading to performance, instrumentality as performance leading to reward, and valence as the reward’s value. This is a conceptual model of perceptions, not a measured score assigned to the fictional employee."], ["Equity", "Equity theory concerns perceived relationships between inputs and outcomes and comparisons with others. The relevant comparison is not simply whether everyone receives the same amount. Investigate workload, contribution, opportunities, outcomes, and the employee’s understanding before assuming either fairness or unfairness."], ["Other process approaches", "Reinforcement examines how consequences shape behavior. Goal-setting emphasizes the direction supplied by clear goals and feedback, with attention to commitment and capability. Positive reinforcement adds a desirable consequence; negative reinforcement removes an aversive condition and is not the same as punishment. Consider whether a reward encourages the intended behavior or an unintended shortcut."]], "question": {"prompt": "An employee wants to edit the display’s audio but lacks working software and practice time. What directly addresses the problem described?", "choices": ["Promise a larger recognition award without changing resources.", "Provide usable software, guided practice, and time to learn.", "Assume the employee does not value the display."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Recognition concerns an outcome. It does not by itself make the task achievable with the resources described.", "Yes. This strengthens the effort-to-performance link, or expectancy. Check afterward whether the support works.", "The scenario says the employee wants to learn. Investigate barriers rather than inventing a motive."]}}, {"id": "leadership", "title": "Match leadership to the work and situation.", "short": "Contingency leadership", "chapter": "CHAPTER 14 / ORGANIZATIONAL LEADERSHIP", "phase": 1, "takeaway": "Contingency thinking asks what fits this situation. Fiedler and path-goal theory make different assumptions.", "narration": "Leadership influences people toward a purpose. Formal authority can help, but a title alone does not create trust or good judgment. Contingency theories examine the fit among leader, people, task, and setting. Fiedler's model treats leadership style as relatively fixed and examines leader-member relations, task structure, and position power. Its central question is how to match style and situation. House's path-goal theory instead allows leaders to vary their behavior. Directive behavior clarifies the work. Supportive behavior attends to people's needs. Participative behavior involves people in decisions. Achievement-oriented behavior sets challenging goals with confidence and support. For Cedar Crossing's experienced collections specialist, detailed instructions for every familiar step may add little. A colleague new to audio editing may need a clear demonstration and an accessible way to ask questions. The choice depends on the task and person, not a stereotype about age or job title. Explain the purpose, notice what support is needed, and revise the approach as conditions change.", "description": "A comparison separates Fiedler’s relatively fixed style and situational fit from path-goal theory’s adaptable behaviors. Four path-goal behaviors have plain-language definitions; fictional experienced and new-to-task colleagues illustrate different support needs.", "explore": [["Fiedler’s situational variables", "Leader-member relations concern trust and acceptance. Task structure concerns clarity and structure of the work. Position power concerns formal authority. The textbook presents task-oriented leadership as fitting the most and least favorable conditions and relationship-oriented leadership as fitting intermediate conditions. Do not turn this into a universal causal law or describe Fiedler as simply switching style at will."], ["Path-goal theory", "Directive, supportive, participative, and achievement-oriented behaviors can vary with employee characteristics and the work environment. Clarifying a path can include explaining goals, removing obstacles, and connecting performance with appropriate outcomes. Participation is one possible behavior, not the automatic choice for every decision."], ["Styles and evidence", "Autocratic, democratic, and laissez-faire labels describe broad patterns, not sufficient diagnoses. Trait approaches examine leader characteristics; behavioral approaches examine what leaders do. Evaluate the actual actions, context, and effects rather than assuming one label guarantees effectiveness."]]}, {"id": "shared", "title": "Share leadership while keeping responsibility clear.", "short": "Trust and shared leadership", "chapter": "CHAPTER 14 / ORGANIZATIONAL LEADERSHIP", "phase": 1, "takeaway": "Leadership can be distributed across people while accountability, support, and ethical limits remain explicit.", "narration": "A heroic image puts the leader at the center of every success. Shared or distributed leadership recognizes that influence and expertise can come from several people. At Cedar Crossing, a colleague with audio experience might guide a technical decision while another leads discussion of local sources. Sharing leadership still requires clear authority and support. Transactional leadership emphasizes exchanges and agreed performance; transformational leadership emphasizes shared purpose, development, and change. They are not simply bad and good alternatives. Collins's Level Five leadership combines personal humility with professional will, directing ambition toward the organization rather than personal acclaim. Treat that research-based description as a lens, not a promise that one personality guarantees success. Also attend to harmful behavior. Repeated humiliation, intimidation, or suppression of concerns can damage trust and information sharing. Toxic leadership concerns patterns and effects; a disagreement alone does not establish it. Invite informed challenge, give credit accurately, and make it possible to raise concerns through appropriate channels.", "description": "A network of colleagues shows expertise supporting a shared purpose, with a separate boundary for formal authority and accountability. Humility and professional will are paired; a trust panel contrasts constructive challenge with recurring intimidation.", "explore": [["Shared influence and formal responsibility", "Distributed leadership is not the disappearance of managers. Different people can lead particular contributions while the institution retains decision boundaries, accountability, and support. Clear coordination helps prevent overlapping or abandoned responsibilities."], ["Transactional and transformational leadership", "Transactional approaches connect performance and exchanges. Transformational approaches develop commitment to purpose and change. These perspectives can overlap in practice; none eliminates the need to examine ethical conduct and results."], ["Level Five and toxic behavior", "The textbook’s prose describes Collins’s Level Five leadership as personal humility plus professional will. It does not mean passivity or weak standards. Toxic leadership involves harmful patterns such as abuse of power or intimidation; discuss specific evidence and effects rather than diagnosing a personality or using the term for every unpopular decision."]]}, {"id": "ethics", "title": "Examine benefits, rights, and fairness together.", "short": "Professional ethics", "chapter": "CHAPTER 15 / PROFESSIONAL ETHICS", "phase": 2, "takeaway": "Ethical judgment considers affected people, competing values, professional commitments, and defensible reasons.", "narration": "An attractive outcome can still raise ethical questions. Suppose a donated photograph would enrich Cedar Crossing's display, but its description reveals personal information about someone pictured. First establish the facts, the relevant permissions, and who could be affected. Utilitarian reasoning compares benefits and harms across possible actions. An individual-rights approach asks whose dignity, privacy, or other rights must be respected. Social justice asks whether the treatment and distribution of benefits and burdens are fair. These lenses can point in different directions. A popular exhibit does not settle every concern. Martha Smith's model adds four loyalties: self, clients or patrons, the profession, and the employing institution. A manager may have to negotiate tensions among them. Professional codes, including the American Library Association's Code of Ethics, offer principles rather than a ready answer for every situation. Consider alternatives, explain the reasoning, and seek appropriate guidance when needed. Ethical judgment and legal compliance are related, but they are not identical questions.", "description": "Three ethical lenses surround a fictional display decision: benefits and harms, individual rights, and social justice. A separate four-loyalties diagram identifies self, clients or patrons, profession, and employing institution. The photo scenario remains an inquiry, not a legal conclusion.", "explore": [["Normative ethical frameworks", "Utilitarianism evaluates likely benefits and harms of alternatives. Individual rights asks whether affected people’s rights and dignity are respected. Social justice examines fairness, relevant standards, and the distribution of benefits and burdens. Explain which considerations support a decision; do not merely attach a theory name to a preferred outcome."], ["Martha Smith’s multiple loyalties", "The four categories in the assigned text are self, clients or patrons, profession, and employing institution. Loyalty to the profession concerns professional responsibilities and standards; it is broader than loyalty to a professional association. Conflicting loyalties require judgment rather than an automatic ranking that fits every case."], ["Professional code and context", "The current ALA Code of Ethics addresses service, intellectual freedom, privacy, intellectual property, fair treatment of colleagues, conflicts of interest, professional duties, development, and dignity and justice. The code was amended in 2021, after this textbook edition. It provides broad principles and explicitly does not dictate conduct for every situation. See the source link below; no legal determination about the fictional photograph is supplied."]], "question": {"prompt": "A colleague says a photo should be displayed because visitors will love it. What would strengthen the ethical analysis?", "choices": ["Treat popularity as the only relevant evidence.", "Assume donation resolves every question about use.", "Examine affected people, relevant permissions, rights, harms, and fairness."], "answer": 2, "feedback": ["Audience benefit matters in a consequences analysis, but it does not settle competing values or missing facts.", "Donation alone does not establish every relevant fact or ethical obligation in this hypothetical.", "Yes. This brings several ethical lenses and missing facts into the decision instead of assuming one desirable outcome settles it."]}}, {"id": "communication", "title": "A sent message is only part of communication.", "short": "Meaning, channels, and feedback", "chapter": "CHAPTER 16 / ORGANIZATIONAL COMMUNICATION", "phase": 3, "takeaway": "Check shared understanding through feedback. Match the channel to the message and the people receiving it.", "narration": "Communication involves creating and interpreting meaning. A sender encodes a message, uses a channel, and a receiver decodes it. Feedback helps test what was understood. Noise is anything that hinders the process, not only sound. Jargon, assumptions, interruptions, or an inaccessible format can interfere. At Cedar Crossing, the instruction make the captions accessible sounds clear until colleagues discover they mean different things by accessible. A shared example and a check-back can reveal the difference. Downward communication moves from supervisors to staff; upward communication carries information and concerns toward supervisors. Horizontal communication connects colleagues at the same organizational level. Informal networks also carry useful information, but important decisions still need reliable confirmation. Choose a channel suited to the task. A written specification may support reference later; a conversation may help resolve ambiguity. Listen, ask for clarification, and confirm the agreed meaning. Sending another copy of the same unclear message may simply repeat the original problem.", "description": "A communication loop shows sender, encoding, message and channel, decoding, receiver, and feedback, with noise affecting the process. Separate labeled arrows identify downward, upward, and horizontal flows. An ambiguous caption instruction illustrates the need for shared examples.", "explore": [["Flow directions", "Downward: supervisor to staff, including direction and information. Upward: staff to supervisor, including feedback, concerns, and suggestions. Horizontal: exchange among colleagues at the same level. The assigned text also groups some lateral or diagonal exchanges under horizontal communication; actual diagonal exchanges may cross both unit and hierarchical boundaries. Focus on the relationship described rather than an arrow’s visual angle."], ["Barriers and active listening", "People may filter information, interpret words differently, miss nonverbal cues, or face overload, status barriers, and inaccessible channels. Active listening involves attention, clarification, and checking interpretation. Agreement is not required to understand someone’s message accurately."], ["Formal and informal channels", "Formal channels follow recognized arrangements; informal communication arises through relationships. Informal information may be timely but still needs verification. An official channel can also carry ambiguity or error. Preserve an accessible shared record of important decisions without assuming that one email guarantees understanding."]], "question": {"prompt": "Two colleagues interpret “accessible captions” differently. What best checks their shared understanding?", "choices": ["Compare a concrete example and ask each person to explain the agreed requirements.", "Send the same sentence again with an urgent subject line.", "Assume silence means everyone agrees."], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Yes. An example and check-back reveal how the message was decoded and create useful feedback.", "Urgency does not explain the ambiguous term. Repetition alone leaves the meaning unresolved.", "Silence may reflect uncertainty, workload, or reluctance to question. It does not establish shared understanding."]}}, {"id": "conflict", "title": "Explore the concern behind the position.", "short": "Listening and conflict", "chapter": "CHAPTER 16 / ORGANIZATIONAL COMMUNICATION", "phase": 3, "takeaway": "Use the issue, stakes, time, and relationship to choose a response to conflict.", "narration": "Disagreement can reveal information a group needs. It can also become damaging when people attack one another or stop listening. Cedar Crossing colleagues disagree about long historical captions versus a simpler display. One wants to preserve context; the other wants visitors to understand the main point quickly. Those underlying concerns create more room for problem-solving than the demand that one person win. The chapter describes competing, accommodating, avoiding, compromising, and collaborating responses. Their usefulness depends on the issue and conditions. Collaboration explores a solution that addresses important interests, but it takes time and is not a universal response to every urgent situation. A compromise shares concessions; it may be workable without fully satisfying either concern. Listen to the evidence, clarify the decision, and agree how to move forward. Test a small display sample if that would inform the choice. The proposal is hypothetical: we still need evidence about what visitors can understand and use.", "description": "Two positions about caption length are connected to underlying concerns: historical context and immediate usability. Five conflict responses are compared by assertiveness and cooperation in an accessible text description; a proposed small test is labeled hypothetical.", "explore": [["Five conflict responses", "Competing emphasizes one’s own position; accommodating yields to another; avoiding postpones or withdraws; compromising exchanges concessions; collaborating explores an approach that addresses important interests. These are responses to situations, not permanent personality labels."], ["Choose for the conditions", "Consider the significance of the issue, time available, authority, safety, relationships, and the information needed. A temporary pause may allow better preparation; avoiding an important unresolved problem indefinitely may worsen it. Collaboration requires a workable process and willingness to engage."], ["Conflict and professionalism", "Keep discussion focused on evidence and effects. Make room for relevant disagreement without treating disrespect or intimidation as necessary debate. Do not ask students to disclose personal workplace conflicts to complete this lesson."]]}, {"id": "empowerment", "title": "Give participation a clear scope and real support.", "short": "Empowerment and decisions", "chapter": "CHAPTER 17 / EMPOWERING EMPLOYEES THROUGH THE USE OF TEAMS", "phase": 4, "takeaway": "Empowerment joins meaningful influence with information, capabilities, resources, and accountability.", "narration": "Empowerment expands people's ability to influence and carry out their work. Participation invites input into decisions. Neither works well when the invitation hides fixed decisions or leaves people without information and resources. Cedar Crossing's project group can propose the display sequence within agreed space and access requirements. The manager explains which choices the group may make, which need approval, and where to get help. The group also needs relevant skills, usable information, time, and a clear result. A small decision agreement can identify purpose, participants, decision authority, process, and follow-up. Different decisions may call for consultation, delegated choice, voting, or consensus. Define the process before relying on it; a vote and consensus are not interchangeable. Self-management means taking on management responsibilities within an agreed scope. It does not mean that support and accountability disappear. Evaluate whether participation improves both the work and people's ability to contribute, and change arrangements when the evidence shows a problem.", "description": "A fictional decision agreement specifies the display purpose, participants, authority, boundaries, resources, and follow-up. Participation, delegated choice, voting, and consensus are distinguished. A support connection remains visible between the group and the manager.", "explore": [["Empowerment and participative management", "Participative management involves employees in decisions. Empowerment concerns meaningful discretion and the capacity to act. Information, competence, resources, trust, and appropriate accountability help make discretion usable. A suggestion box alone does not establish empowerment."], ["An illustrative decision agreement", "For Cedar Crossing, specify what the group is trying to achieve, whose expertise is needed, who can decide, relevant limits, how the decision will be made, what help is available, and how the result will be reviewed. This is an original teaching aid, not a prescribed remedy for Riverside."], ["Group and team arrangements", "A team coordinates interdependent contributions toward a shared purpose; a group may have less integrated work. Problem-solving groups, cross-functional teams, virtual teams, and self-managed teams differ in purpose and authority. Self-managed teams assume defined responsibilities such as organizing aspects of their work; actual arrangements vary."]], "question": {"prompt": "The project group may choose the display sequence but is unsure which access requirements are fixed. What makes its decision authority usable?", "choices": ["Require the group to guess, so it learns independence.", "Clarify the boundaries and give access to the information and help needed.", "Invite suggestions while keeping every choice secretly predetermined."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Guessing about essential boundaries can undermine both the service and the group’s confidence.", "Yes. Clear boundaries and practical support make delegated discretion meaningful.", "Participation is weakened when the stated opportunity to influence the decision is not real."]}}, {"id": "teams", "title": "Build team conditions and review how they work.", "short": "Team development and adaptation", "chapter": "CHAPTER 17 / EMPOWERING EMPLOYEES THROUGH THE USE OF TEAMS", "phase": 4, "takeaway": "Team development is a useful lens, not a timetable or a reason to ignore service problems.", "narration": "Effective teams need relevant skills, mutual trust, appropriate size, good communication, appropriate leadership, clear goals, and loyalty to the shared work. Loyalty does not require silence about a weak idea. Tuckman's model describes forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning. Forming establishes purpose and relationships. Storming involves challenges over direction and control. Norming develops shared expectations. Performing focuses energy on the task. Adjourning concerns closure when a temporary team's work ends. These stages offer a way to interpret patterns; they do not give every team a fixed timetable or guarantee that problems resolve without action. Watch for social loafing, when individuals reduce effort in a group, and groupthink, when pressure for agreement crowds out critical evaluation. At Cedar Crossing, the temporary display group needs clear contributions and room for informed disagreement. Contingency management brings the lesson together: examine the task, people, and setting, choose an approach, and review its effects on staff and service.", "description": "A five-stage ribbon labels forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning, with plain descriptions and a note that the model is not a fixed timetable. Seven conditions for effective teamwork surround a review loop. Social loafing and groupthink are clearly distinguished.", "explore": [["The five-stage model", "Forming concerns purpose, structure, and relationships. Storming involves questions about direction and control. Norming develops shared expectations and identity. Performing focuses collective energy on accomplishing the work. Adjourning concerns closure for a time-limited team. Name a stage only when evidence supports the interpretation; different patterns may coexist. The textbook cites Tuckman and Jensen’s 1977 extension for the five-stage model."], ["Seven characteristics in the chapter prose", "Relevant skills; mutual trust; appropriate size; good communication; appropriate leadership; clear goals; loyalty. The chapter’s figure omits leadership, but its prose includes it. No single team size guarantees success. Leadership may come from team members, with managers coaching or facilitating as appropriate."], ["Group dynamics and contingency", "Social loafing concerns reduced individual effort in group work; groupthink concerns conformity displacing critical evaluation. Risky shift describes a tendency discussed in the text for some groups to choose more risk than individuals would, not a universal law for all teams. General contingency management integrates the situation across management functions; it is broader than Fiedler’s specific leadership model."]]}, {"id": "case", "title": "Build your argument from the case and the readings.", "short": "Case and discussion preparation", "chapter": "CASE / THE SELF-MANAGED TEAM EXPERIMENT", "phase": 4, "takeaway": "Link each interpretation to case evidence and course concepts, then justify a feasible recommendation.", "narration": "Now read The Self-Managed Team Experiment at Riverside Public Library. Director Maria Santos introduces a team approach in the reference department. The case includes perspectives from Susan Palmer, James Chen, Rachel Martinez, Kevin, and Tom Bradley, along with reported effects on service. Begin by separating described events, participants' interpretations, and information the narrative does not provide. Do not assume that a character's explanation settles a motive or that a stage label settles the response. Use relevant ideas from motivation, leadership, ethics, communication, and teams to explain the evidence. Compare possible actions, consider tradeoffs, and justify your own recommendation with concrete implementation steps. Check whether your argument addresses both staff experience and service needs. Your discussion requires your own thinking and writing, an initial post, and two substantive peer responses. Read the current Canvas directions for the word counts, rubric, and deadline. The lesson and Library Shift offer practice, not a model discussion answer or a diagnosis of the team.", "description": "An analysis sequence separates events, interpretations, and unknowns, then connects evidence to relevant concepts, options, tradeoffs, and the student’s justified recommendation. A final checklist covers the student’s own writing, implementation, APA citations, and peer engagement.", "explore": [["Case boundaries", "The case is set at Riverside Public Library, not Riverside Community College. Maria Santos is director; Susan Palmer remains head of reference. The department includes eight professional librarians and four paraprofessionals. The narrative supplies differing accounts and an observed meeting but no final intervention or result. Do not invent Kevin’s surname, an HR process, a budget amount, complete authority agreements, or a precise event calendar."], ["Discussion requirements from the supplied directions", "Write a 400–700-word initial post in Canvas. Address problem identification, leadership and team dynamics across Chapters 13–17, your recommendation, theory application, and implementation/change strategy. Write at least two substantive peer responses of 75–100 words each. Use professional writing and APA 7 citations. Post your own initial response before reading classmates’ posts. Check the current Canvas rubric and deadline."], ["Use the text precisely", "Explain how a concept accounts for specific evidence and acknowledge its limits. When citing, use chapter titles and verified page numbers from your own edition rather than copying this export’s PDF counter as an original book page. Your case analysis and discussion should reflect your own thinking and writing."], ["Final review checklist", "Have I separated evidence from interpretation? Have I explained relevant concepts rather than only naming them? Have I considered alternative explanations and tradeoffs? Are the proposed steps feasible under stated assumptions? Have I addressed implementation and how results would be reviewed? Have I checked APA citations, word counts, and the two peer responses against Canvas?"]]}], "games": [{"statement": "A colleague asks what the display should help visitors understand. What is the useful first move?", "choices": ["Explain the shared purpose.", "List every possible task.", "Ask them to guess."], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Yes. A clear purpose helps people connect their contributions.", "Tasks matter, but this question asks why the work is being done.", "A shared purpose needs explanation, not guesswork."], "explanation": "Yes. A clear purpose helps people connect their contributions.", "practice": true}, {"statement": "The audio tool fails repeatedly. Staff are frustrated. What addresses the barrier described?", "choices": ["Offer praise alone.", "Repair the tool and check usability.", "Add more editing tasks."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Recognition does not repair an unusable tool.", "Yes. Workable conditions provide a foundation; meaningful work and recognition remain distinct concerns.", "More tasks would add load without addressing the equipment problem."], "explanation": "Yes. Workable conditions provide a foundation; meaningful work and recognition remain distinct concerns."}, {"statement": "An experienced colleague knows the task, but a new colleague needs guidance. What fits path-goal thinking?", "choices": ["Give both no guidance.", "Use identical instructions forever.", "Adapt support to the task and person."], "answer": 2, "feedback": ["New-to-task staff may need clarification and support.", "Identical instructions may not address different needs.", "Yes. Path-goal theory allows different behaviors as needs and conditions change."], "explanation": "Yes. Path-goal theory allows different behaviors as needs and conditions change."}, {"statement": "An exhibit choice benefits visitors but may expose personal information. What should the group examine?", "choices": ["Benefits, rights, and fairness.", "Popularity alone.", "Only the manager’s preference."], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Yes. Consider competing values, missing facts, and affected people.", "Popularity is one possible benefit, not a complete ethical analysis.", "A personal preference does not substitute for ethical reasoning."], "explanation": "Yes. Consider competing values, missing facts, and affected people.", "golden": true}, {"statement": "Two colleagues interpret the same instruction differently. What helps identify the misunderstanding?", "choices": ["Send it again unchanged.", "Treat silence as agreement.", "Ask for a check-back with an example."], "answer": 2, "feedback": ["Repeating ambiguity may repeat the problem.", "Silence does not establish shared understanding.", "Yes. Feedback reveals how the message was interpreted."], "explanation": "Yes. Feedback reveals how the message was interpreted."}, {"statement": "A group avoids examining a weak idea because nobody wants disagreement. What should a facilitator encourage?", "choices": ["Immediate agreement.", "Respectful critical evaluation.", "Silence from uncertain members."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Quick agreement can conceal important information.", "Yes. Critical evaluation helps counter groupthink while preserving respect.", "Silencing uncertainty can strengthen pressure to conform."], "explanation": "Yes. Critical evaluation helps counter groupthink while preserving respect."}, {"statement": "The group has a decision to make but no access to necessary information. What supports empowerment?", "choices": ["Provide information and clarify authority.", "Announce that support is over.", "Keep the decision rules hidden."], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Yes. Meaningful discretion needs information, boundaries, and practical support.", "Withdrawing help does not make a group capable of deciding.", "Hidden rules weaken meaningful influence."], "explanation": "Yes. Meaningful discretion needs information, boundaries, and practical support.", "golden": true}, {"statement": "A temporary project ends. What makes its closure useful?", "choices": ["Skip discussion of the work.", "Review learning and arrange handoffs.", "Assume everyone knows what happens next."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Closure can preserve learning and clarify responsibilities.", "Yes. Adjourning includes attention to closure; a review and handoff can support future work.", "Unstated assumptions can leave work and learning unresolved."], "explanation": "Yes. Adjourning includes attention to closure; a review and handoff can support future work."}, {"statement": "A manager labels a group “storming.” What must still happen?", "choices": ["Wait without reviewing service.", "Treat the label as a full diagnosis.", "Check evidence and needed support."], "answer": 2, "feedback": ["A stage label does not justify neglecting service problems.", "A label organizes interpretation but does not settle causes or responses.", "Yes. Examine the actual conditions, effects, and support needed."], "explanation": "Yes. Examine the actual conditions, effects, and support needed."}], "visuals": {"welcome": "<div class=\"scene-block\"><img src=\"cedar-display.svg\" width=\"1120\" height=\"760\" alt=\"Colleagues prepare a display with photographs, captions, and an audio station.\"><div><span class=\"small-label\">FICTIONAL PROJECT</span><h2>A community history display</h2><p>Collections knowledge, audio skills, clear writing, and visitor access all matter.</p><p><strong>Shared purpose:</strong> help visitors understand and explore local history.</p></div></div><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "motivation": "<h2>Workable conditions + meaningful work</h2><p class=\"intro-line\">Herzberg’s distinction is a lens for inquiry.</p><div class=\"lead-panels\"><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"repairing an unreliable\"><h3>Remove a barrier</h3><p>Repair the unreliable audio station. Pay, supervision, and conditions also deserve attention.</p></div><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"meaningful responsibility\"><h3>Enrich the work</h3><p>Give an interested colleague meaningful responsibility for the listening experience.</p></div></div><div class=\"lead-rows\"><div><strong>Ask the person</strong><span>What matters to you, and what makes the task workable?</span></div><div><strong>Check the evidence</strong><span>Neither action guarantees the same response from every worker.</span></div></div><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "expectancy": "<h2>Three perceptions connect the effort</h2><ol class=\"lead-steps\"><li><strong>Effort → performance</strong><span>Expectancy: Can I do the work with effort and support?</span></li><li><strong>Performance → outcome</strong><span>Instrumentality: Will performance lead to the promised outcome?</span></li><li><strong>Value of outcome</strong><span>Valence: Does that outcome matter to me?</span></li></ol><div class=\"signal lead-callout\" data-signal=\"neither working software\"><p><strong>Missing software or practice time?</strong> Strengthen the effort-to-performance link.</p></div><div class=\"lead-rows\"><div><strong>Equity is a separate lens</strong><span>How do I compare my contributions and outcomes with a relevant other?</span></div></div><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "leadership": "<h2>Two different contingency models</h2><div class=\"lead-panels\"><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"Fiedler&#x27;s model\"><h3>Fiedler</h3><p>Relatively fixed style. Examine leader-member relations, task structure, and position power. Match style and situation.</p></div><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"House&#x27;s path-goal\"><h3>Path-goal</h3><p>Adapt behavior to the people and work. Clarify the path and remove obstacles.</p></div></div><div class=\"lead-rows\"><div><strong>Directive</strong><span>Clarify the work.</span></div><div><strong>Supportive</strong><span>Attend to people’s needs.</span></div><div><strong>Participative</strong><span>Involve people in decisions.</span></div><div><strong>Achievement-oriented</strong><span>Set challenging goals with confidence and support.</span></div></div><p class=\"visual-note\">Experienced in this task: detailed instructions may add little. New to this task: a demonstration may help.</p><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "shared": "<h2>Expertise can lead from several places</h2><div class=\"expertise-network\"><div><strong>Collections knowledge</strong><span>Guide source interpretation</span></div><div class=\"shared-purpose\"><strong>Shared purpose</strong><span>A useful community display</span></div><div><strong>Audio experience</strong><span>Guide technical choices</span></div></div><p class=\"authority-band\">Formal authority, coordination, support, and accountability remain explicit.</p><div class=\"lead-panels\"><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"personal humility\"><h3>Level Five</h3><p>Personal humility + professional will. Ambition serves the organization.</p></div><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"Repeated humiliation\"><h3>Protect trust</h3><p>Invite informed challenge. Address recurring intimidation and suppression of concerns.</p></div></div><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "ethics": "<h2>Examine a decision through three lenses</h2><p class=\"intro-line\">A donated photograph may enrich the display while revealing personal information.</p><div class=\"lead-panels\"><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"Utilitarian reasoning\"><h3>Benefits and harms</h3><p>Compare likely consequences for affected people.</p></div><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"individual-rights\"><h3>Individual rights</h3><p>Examine dignity, privacy, and relevant rights.</p></div><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"Social justice\"><h3>Social justice</h3><p>Examine fairness and the distribution of benefits and burdens.</p></div></div><h3 class=\"space-above\">Martha Smith’s four loyalties</h3><ul class=\"loyalties\"><li>Self</li><li>Clients or patrons</li><li>Profession</li><li>Employing institution</li></ul><p class=\"visual-note\">Establish facts and permissions. The hypothetical is not a legal conclusion.</p><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "communication": "<h2>Close the communication loop</h2><ol class=\"lead-steps\"><li><strong>Sender</strong><span>Encode the intended meaning.</span></li><li><strong>Message + channel</strong><span>Use a form people can access and interpret.</span></li><li><strong>Receiver</strong><span>Decode and interpret the message.</span></li></ol><p class=\"feedback-arrow\">← Feedback returns to the sender: check what was understood.</p><p class=\"noise-band\"><strong>Noise can affect any part:</strong> jargon, assumptions, interruptions, inaccessible formats.</p><div class=\"lead-rows\"><div><strong>Downward ↓</strong><span>Supervisor to staff</span></div><div><strong>Upward ↑</strong><span>Staff to supervisor</span></div><div><strong>Horizontal ↔</strong><span>Colleagues at the same level</span></div></div><div class=\"signal lead-callout\" data-signal=\"shared example\"><p><strong>“Make captions accessible.”</strong> Compare an example and ask for a check-back.</p></div><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "conflict": "<h2>Move from positions to underlying concerns</h2><div class=\"lead-panels\"><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"preserve context\"><h3>“Keep more historical detail.”</h3><p>Concern: preserve context.</p></div><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"understand the main point\"><h3>“Make the captions shorter.”</h3><p>Concern: help visitors understand quickly.</p></div></div><div class=\"lead-rows\"><div><strong>Competing</strong><span>High assertiveness; low cooperation.</span></div><div><strong>Accommodating</strong><span>Low assertiveness; high cooperation.</span></div><div><strong>Avoiding</strong><span>Low assertiveness and cooperation.</span></div><div><strong>Compromising</strong><span>Intermediate assertiveness and cooperation; exchange concessions.</span></div><div><strong>Collaborating</strong><span>High assertiveness and cooperation; explore important interests.</span></div></div><p class=\"lead-callout\"><strong>Hypothetical next step:</strong> test a small display sample if it would inform the choice. No visitor result is assumed.</p><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "empowerment": "<div class=\"plan-sheet\"><span class=\"small-label\">ILLUSTRATIVE DECISION AGREEMENT</span><h2>Make the group’s discretion usable</h2><dl><dt>Purpose</dt><dd>A useful community history display.</dd><dt>Participants</dt><dd>Relevant collections, audio, writing, and access expertise.</dd><dt>Authority</dt><dd>Clarify which sequence choices the group can make and which need approval.</dd><dt>Boundaries</dt><dd>Agreed space and access requirements.</dd><dt>Resources</dt><dd>Information, time, skills, equipment, and help.</dd><dt>Follow-up</dt><dd>Review how the arrangement affects work and participation.</dd></dl></div><p class=\"authority-band\">Group ↔ manager: support and accountability continue.</p><div class=\"lead-rows\"><div><strong>Participation</strong><span>Input into a decision.</span></div><div><strong>Delegated choice</strong><span>Decision authority within an agreed scope.</span></div><div><strong>Voting</strong><span>A defined tally determines the choice.</span></div><div><strong>Consensus</strong><span>An agreed process seeks a decision members can support; not simply a vote.</span></div></div><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "teams": "<h2>Interpret development without a fixed timetable</h2><ol class=\"lead-steps\"><li><strong>Forming</strong><span>Purpose and relationships</span></li><li><strong>Storming</strong><span>Direction and control</span></li><li><strong>Norming</strong><span>Shared expectations</span></li><li><strong>Performing</strong><span>Energy focused on the task</span></li><li><strong>Adjourning</strong><span>Closure and handoffs</span></li></ol><h3 class=\"space-above\">Seven conditions to review</h3><ul class=\"conditions\"><li>Relevant skills</li><li>Mutual trust</li><li>Appropriate size</li><li>Good communication</li><li>Appropriate leadership</li><li>Clear goals</li><li>Loyalty to shared work</li></ul><p class=\"feedback-arrow\">Examine conditions → choose an approach → review effects → examine again.</p><div class=\"lead-panels\"><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"social loafing\"><h3>Social loafing</h3><p>Reduced individual effort in group work.</p></div><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"groupthink\"><h3>Groupthink</h3><p>Pressure for agreement crowds out critical evaluation.</p></div></div><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>", "case": "<h2>Keep evidence and interpretation distinct</h2><div class=\"lead-panels\"><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"described events\"><h3>Events</h3><p>What does the narrative describe?</p></div><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"participants&#x27; interpretations\"><h3>Interpretations</h3><p>What do people say an event means?</p></div><div class=\"signal \" data-signal=\"does not provide\"><h3>Unknowns</h3><p>What information remains unavailable?</p></div></div><ol class=\"lead-steps\"><li><strong>Explain</strong><span>Link relevant concepts to specific evidence.</span></li><li><strong>Compare</strong><span>Consider alternatives and tradeoffs.</span></li><li><strong>Justify</strong><span>Develop your own recommendation and implementation steps.</span></li></ol><div class=\"lead-rows\"><div><strong>Initial post</strong><span>400–700 words; own thinking and writing.</span></div><div><strong>Peer engagement</strong><span>At least two substantive replies, 75–100 words each.</span></div><div><strong>Final checks</strong><span>APA 7 citations, implementation, current Canvas rubric and deadline.</span></div></div><p class=\"visual-note\">No team-stage diagnosis or model case answer is supplied here.</p><p class=\"visual-note\">Cedar Crossing is fictional. Proposed actions are teaching examples.</p>"}};
